>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_generator_family`

``` hol4
Refute.register_generator_family :
  {tyop : {Thy : string, Tyop : string}, constructors : term list,
   canonical : term_postprocessor option} -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Registers a Quickcheck generator for every instance of a type operator.

The generator is built from constructor constants whose declared result
type is `tyop` applied to distinct type variables (e.g. `FEMPTY` and
`FUPDATE` for `:'a |-> 'b`).  Unlike `Refute.register_generator`,
nothing is stored per instance: `tyop` is matched on demand, so the
registration fires for a concrete type never seen before, and the
generator, cardinality, and enumeration caches of every instance already
cached are invalidated.  The generator is never exhaustive, so QC cannot
report `NoCounterexample` for a goal quantified over such a type; only a
closed fact free of such a variable can be decided that way.

`canonical`, if present, is a display-only postprocessor for reported
bindings at that type, applied after testing and certification have run
on the raw term.  It is a second source feeding the one walk
`Refute.register_term_postprocessor` drives, composing with every
pattern-registered postprocessor.  It should remove structure a later
part of the same candidate overwrote (collapsing `FEMPTY |+ (1,2) |+
(1,3)` to `FEMPTY |+ (1,3)`); it need not identify every pair of terms
denoting the same value.

### Failure

Raises `Fail` if `constructors` is empty, a constructor is not a
constant or its result type is not `tyop` applied to distinct type
variables, or `tyop` already has `TypeBase` datatype constructors (a
family there would silently disable finite exhaustion for a possibly
finite type).

### See also

[`Refute.register_generator`](#Refute.register_generator),
[`Refute.abstract_generator`](#Refute.abstract_generator),
[`Refute.register_term_postprocessor`](#Refute.register_term_postprocessor)

Model reconstruction is described in \ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
